    "body": "Guarulhos Airport © Leonid Andronov\nBy Alex Lennane\n25 November 2026\nBrazil’s busiest international air cargo hub is struggling to move freight after tighter export inspections filled its warehouse, leaving some trucks waiting days to unload and delaying the handover of imports that had already cleared customs.\nKuehne+Nagel said on Tuesday that some trucks which arrived at São Paulo’s Guarulhos Airport on Friday 20 November were still waiting to unload. Some airlines had been unable to collect freight from the terminal, leaving less room for new shipments.\nThe export disruption comes on top of recurring problems with import collection. In June, the airport warned that imports were arriving faster than cleared freight was being collected, tying up warehouse space and slowing receiving, storage and handover. It flagged the same imbalance again on 12 November.\nBrazilian forwarder VL Cargo said on Monday that acceptance of new export freight had been temporarily suspended and advised customers to hold deliveries. The airport said that day its terminal remained open around the clock and asked hauliers to confirm a slot before sending trucks. It said perishables, live animals and special cargo were being handled as usual and gave no recovery date.\nLufthansa Cargo told customers it would rebook outstanding weekend shipments, Brazilian trade outlet FUP News reported. The carrier said the terminal had temporarily stopped handing general cargo over to airlines and halted acceptance of new freight into storage when the warehouse reached capacity.\nKuehne+Nagel said the disruption followed a Federal Police operation from 18 to 22 November and that police and customs officers were continuing inspections at cargo acceptance points and in airline storage areas. Brazil’s Federal Police announced an anti-trafficking operation at Guarulhos on 18 November. The airport attributed the backlog to intensified checks on dry export cargo, without identifying a specific operation.\nGuarulhos accounted for 59% of Brazil’s air exports and 55% of its air imports in 2025, according to the airport operator. The cargo terminal reported a record 99,500 tonnes in the third quarter of 2026, up 14.4% year on year, excluding courier shipments. Automotive freight was the largest segment at 20%, followed by pharmaceuticals, chemicals and hospital supplies at 19%, and machinery at 12%.\nFruit and eggs accounted for 36% of Guarulhos’s air export tonnage in 2024, with papayas and mangoes making up nearly four-fifths of fruit shipments. Electrical and electronic goods and chemical industry products, including medicines, were the two largest import categories by weight, according to São Paulo state’s logistics plan.",
